-- #1111 A-2.6 REGRESSION GUARD, the STDLIB reach of the same defect the module
-- corpus pins as `llvm_fixtures_modules/ctor_scope_local_decl_wins/`.
--
-- `import json.{Json, JNull}` names a constructor (`JNull`), so the import-scoped
-- constructor overlay contributes stdlib `json`'s whole `Json` decl — including the
-- sibling `JString`, which this file never imports and which is a perfectly
-- ordinary identifier a user may declare.  This file declares it, on its own `Tok`.
-- A module's own top-level declaration is that module's name (SHADOW-SEMANTICS §1),
-- so `JString "hi"` here is `Tok`'s.
--
-- Expected output, derived by hand BEFORE any capture and independently of any
-- engine: `untok (JString "hi")` is `hi`.  Pinned absolutely in
-- `test/engine_value_pins/engine/ctor_scope_local_ctor_beats_stdlib_import.pin`, so
-- three agreeing-but-wrong engines cannot satisfy it.
--
-- ⚠️ THE FAILURE THIS PINS, measured on the first cut of A-2.6 (PR #1282 as first
-- pushed, binary built from f6c2d391): REJECTED, `Type mismatch: Json vs Tok`, exit
-- 1, three diagnostics — the user's own constructor clobbered by a sibling
-- constructor of a stdlib type the program never named.  Pristine `main`
-- (26585d3c) exits 0 and prints `hi`.
--
-- ⚠️ COUPLING, stated rather than discovered later: this fixture depends on
-- `stdlib/json.mdk` declaring `Json` with a `JNull` and a `JString`.  If those are
-- renamed, re-point the import — do not delete the fixture; the property is that a
-- LOCAL constructor outranks an imported sibling, and the stdlib is only the
-- sharpest available witness that the defect was not confined to test modules.
import json.{Json, JNull}

data Tok = JString String

untok : Tok -> String
untok (JString s) = s

main : <IO> Unit
main = println (untok (JString "hi"))
